
  The first fork decides nearly everything downstream, so here's the comparison before I ask:

  [1mThe fork:[0m where task state lives, and therefore what a "notification" can physically be.

  - [1mExisting backend/API elsewhere[0m — notifications can be real: server-side change detection, delivery while you're
    logged out, email/push all on the table. Buys the full feature; costs an integration contract I'd need to see.
  - [1mGreenfield, backend planned[0m — the honest version of your request, but it means we're designing the whole stack.
    Notifications is then sub-project #4 of 4, and today's spec is really about tasks + persistence. Costs the most;
    rules out nothing.
  - [1mBrowser-only, single user[0m — "notifications" degrades to in-page toasts plus maybe the Web Notifications API for
    same-device alerts. Cheap and shippable this week; rules out cross-device, offline, and multi-user entirely — and
    "users" (plural) stops meaning anything.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me it's the first one if a backend exists, because that changes the design completely and I'd
  rather read its contract than guess. Absent that, I'd push toward the third for now — the smallest thing that makes
  "notify me when a task changes" real — and treat multi-user as a later, separate spec. Building a notification bus on
  top of a nonexistent task model is how you get a subsystem with nothing to subscribe to.

